            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Compares two shapes - two sets of matching points - after taking away everything that is not shape: where they are, how big they are and which way they are turned. The difference that is left is 0 for the same shape and grows the more they differ.\n\nUse it to compare poses or gestures regardless of where a performer stands, how tall they are or which way they face; to match a pose against a library of reference poses;\nto measure how a group's formation keeps or loses its shape as it moves.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.procrustes\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nshape:\nThe shape to compare: rows of coordinates (x y, or x y z, ...). Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nreference:\nThe shape to compare it with, the same number of rows, held. Row n of each must be the same point (the same joint, the same marker).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndifference:\nHow different the shapes are once position, size and turn are taken away: 0 for the same shape, up to 1.\n\naligned:\nThe shape, moved, scaled and turned to fit the reference as well as it can (in the reference's standardized form).\n\nreference standardized:\nThe reference centred and scaled to size 1, for comparison with aligned. Sent first, then aligned, then difference.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a right-angled triangle, and the same triangle turned 40 degrees, three times as big and moved: difference 0. Bottom: a different triangle against the same reference: difference about 0.42. A metro re-sends the shapes twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.align_vectors finds the turn between two sets of points, keeping their size. np.linalg.norm of the difference of two poses compares them without any alignment.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Compares two shapes - two sets of matching points - after taking away everything that is not shape: where they are, how big they are and which way they are turned. The difference that is left is 0 for the same shape and grows the more they differ.\n\nUse it to compare poses or gestures regardless of where a performer stands, how tall they are or which way they face; to match a pose against a library of reference poses;\nto measure how a group's formation keeps or loses its shape as it moves.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.procrustes\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nshape:\nThe shape to compare: rows of coordinates (x y, or x y z, ...). Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nreference:\nThe shape to compare it with, the same number of rows, held. Row n of each must be the same point (the same joint, the same marker).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ndifference:\nHow different the shapes are once position, size and turn are taken away: 0 for the same shape, up to 1.\n\naligned:\nThe shape, moved, scaled and turned to fit the reference as well as it can (in the reference's standardized form).\n\nreference standardized:\nThe reference centred and scaled to size 1, for comparison with aligned. Sent first, then aligned, then difference.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: a right-angled triangle, and the same triangle turned 40 degrees, three times as big and moved: difference 0. Bottom: a different triangle against the same reference: difference about 0.42. A metro re-sends the shapes twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.align_vectors finds the turn between two sets of points, keeping their size. np.linalg.norm of the difference of two poses compares them without any alignment."
        },
